  {
    "ghsa_id": "GHSA-897f-g5gm-fmxm",
    "cve_id": "CVE-2026-115889",
    "url": "https://api.github.com/advisories/GHSA-897f-g5gm-fmxm",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-897f-g5gm-fmxm",
    "summary": "Wasmtime: Bit-packing in Cranelift compiler causes silent overflow and miscompilation",
    "description": "## Summary\n\nIn Wasmtime prior to v43, the Cranelift compiler backend bit-packs its SSA value indices in a way that can result in silent overflow in release builds, and result in incorrect value references in the IR resulting in arbitrary miscompilation. A very large Wasm function body, or one that is specially crafted to use many SSA values, can use this to force type-confusion or other arbitrary side-effects and escape the sandbox.\n\nCranelift was changed in Wasmtime v43 to use a full `u32` index, with checks if that limit is exceeded, and so is no longer susceptible to this bug.\n\n## Impact\n\nA Wasmtime engine with default settings (using the Cranelift backend) on any architecture/platform could experience a sandbox escape from a specially-crafted Wasm module.\n\n## Patches\n\nWasmtime v36.0.19 has been released which fixes this issue.",
    "type": "reviewed",
    "severity": "critical",
    "repository_advisory_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/security-advisories/GHSA-897f-g5gm-fmxm",
