    "summary": "obsidian-tc HTTP transport lacks Origin/DNS-rebinding protection, enabling drive-by vault access on loopback",
    "description": "### Impact\nThe Streamable HTTP transport was constructed without `enableDnsRebindingProtection`/`allowedHosts`/`allowedOrigins` and performed no `Origin`-header validation. With the documented `auth.mode: \"none\"` loopback default, a **drive-by web page could `POST http://127.0.0.1/mcp`** and receive caller `http-local` with scopes `[\"*\"]` — full vault read/write — via DNS rebinding / a forged Origin.\n\n### Patches\nFixed in **1.3.1**. Upgrade to `>= 1.3.1`. The transport now enables DNS-rebinding protection and allowed hosts/origins and returns HTTP 403 on an invalid Origin (per MCP 2025-11-25).\n\n### Workarounds\nDo not expose the HTTP transport with `auth.mode: \"none\"`; require JWT auth, or bind to a non-loopback interface behind a proxy that validates `Origin`.",
